    "slug": "cursor-pagination",
    "primaryQuery": "next_token pagination loop oura whoop api code",
    "h1": "Recipe: Walk next_token Pagination Without Loops or Gaps",
    "metaTitle": "Recipe: next_token Pagination for Oura and WHOOP",
    "metaDescription": "Tested JavaScript that walks Oura and WHOOP next_token pages to the end: same query each page, loop and budget guards, and resume from the last saved page.",
    "updated": "2026-11-05",
    "answer": "A dependency-free async generator that walks a next_token-paginated collection to its last page. Between pages it changes only the cursor parameter, so WHOOP's start and end ride along unchanged as WHOOP requires; it stops on a null, missing or empty token; it refuses to continue when a token comes back twice or the page budget runs out; and it hands each page's next URL to a callback so a crashed walk resumes where it stopped. It ships the two cursor shapes the site's guides document: Oura's data array with next_token sent back as next_token, and WHOOP's next_token sent back as nextToken with a maximum page size of 25.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "What request parameter carries the WHOOP API's next_token on the next page?",
        "a": "nextToken. WHOOP's collection responses return the cursor in a field named next_token, and its pagination guide says to place that token on the subsequent request using the same query parameters; the site's WHOOP guide spells the request parameter nextToken. So a request with start and end must keep both exactly as they were and add nextToken, and the walk ends when next_token comes back empty. Sending next_token as the parameter name, the way Oura's API expects, is an easy mistake when one client serves both vendors, and the recipe's test fails if the WHOOP cursor ever sends it."
      },
      {
        "q": "How do I stop a pagination loop when an API keeps returning the same cursor?",
        "a": "Remember every token you have followed and stop the moment one repeats. A cursor that comes back twice means the upstream is replaying a page, or your request is not sending the token it thinks it is, and either way continuing is an infinite loop billed against your rate limit. The recipe keeps a set of seen tokens, including the one in a resume URL, and throws a PaginationLoopError naming the token and page. It also enforces a page budget that throws a PageBudgetError carrying the URL to resume from, so a long walk can stop cleanly and pick up later."
      },
      {
        "q": "Where should a resumable pagination walk save its checkpoint?",
        "a": "After the page has been processed, not when it is fetched. The recipe yields each page to your loop body first and calls onPage only when control comes back, so a crash while you are writing page three leaves the checkpoint pointing at page three, and the resumed walk reads it again instead of skipping it. That makes the processing of a page at-least-once, so the write must be idempotent: upsert on the record's id rather than appending. The saved value is the full next URL, which carries the original query and the cursor together, so resuming needs nothing else."
      }
    ],
