    "h1": "Recipe: A Multi-Window Rate Pacer for Fitness APIs",
    "metaTitle": "Recipe: Multi-Window Rate Pacer for Strava and WHOOP",
    "metaDescription": "Tested JavaScript that paces requests under every window a vendor enforces at once: Strava's quarter-hour and daily limits, WHOOP's and Wahoo's.",
    "updated": "2026-11-05",
    "answer": "A dependency-free request pacer that checks each call against every window a vendor enforces at once and waits for the tightest one, instead of discovering the limit through 429s. Strava's windows are fixed, resetting on the quarter hour and at midnight UTC, with a separate non-upload limit; WHOOP's and Wahoo's are paced as sliding windows, which is safe whatever boundary the vendor uses. Presets carry only published numbers: Strava's 200 per 15 minutes and 2,000 a day overall and 100 and 1,000 for non-upload calls, WHOOP's 100 a minute and 10,000 a day, and Wahoo's sandbox and production limits with the calls Wahoo does not count left uncharged.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Why pace a fitness API client-side when the server already returns 429?",
        "a": "Because a 429 is not a refund. Strava says requests that break the short-term limit still count toward the daily one, so a client that learns its limit by hitting it spends daily budget on refusals, and several workers sharing one app's quota will all hit it together. Pacing before the request keeps every window under its limit by construction and turns the limit into a wait you can schedule around. A reactive 429 handler is still needed underneath, for usage you cannot see, such as another deployment on the same credentials; the site's rate-limit fetcher recipe is that layer."
      },
      {
        "q": "When do Strava's API rate-limit windows reset?",
        "a": "Strava documents a 15-minute window that resets at 0, 15, 30 and 45 minutes past the hour and a daily window that resets at midnight UTC, as the site's Strava guide quotes them from October 17, 2026. They are fixed windows, so 100 non-upload requests sent at 10:14 leave you waiting one minute, not fifteen. The recipe computes each window's start by flooring the epoch time to the window length, which lands on UTC quarter hours and UTC midnight, and it tests both boundaries. Usage headers report a 15-minute and a daily count, and the pacer can adopt them when they are higher than its own."
      },
      {
        "q": "How should a rate pacer handle a vendor that does not say when its windows reset?",
        "a": "Pace it as a sliding window. If no stretch of W milliseconds ever contains more than N of your requests, then no window of length W can contain more than N either, wherever the vendor anchors its boundaries. That costs some throughput at the edges compared with a fixed window you knew about, and buys correctness without a fact you do not have. The recipe uses sliding windows for WHOOP's per-minute and per-day limits and Wahoo's five-minute, hourly and daily limits, and fixed windows only for Strava, whose boundaries are documented."
      }
    ],
