    "h1": "Recipe: Encrypt OAuth Tokens at Rest, With Key Rotation",
    "metaTitle": "Recipe: Encrypt OAuth Tokens at Rest in Node",
    "metaDescription": "Tested Node code that seals fitness API tokens with AES-256-GCM, binds each one to its row so a copied token fails, and rotates keys without downtime.",
    "updated": "2026-11-05",
    "answer": "A dependency-free Node module that encrypts OAuth access and refresh tokens before they reach your database, using AES-256-GCM from node:crypto with a fresh random IV per seal. Each ciphertext is bound to the row it belongs to through GCM's additional authenticated data, so a token copied onto another user's row, or swapped between the access and refresh columns, fails authentication instead of decrypting. Every sealed value names its key id, so rotation is add a key, make it active, and rewrap rows as they are read; opening with a deleted key fails with a reason rather than returning garbage.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Why encrypt fitness API refresh tokens if the database is already encrypted at rest?",
        "a": "Because disk encryption protects against a stolen disk, not against a database dump, a backup copied to the wrong bucket, a read replica with looser access or a SQL injection, all of which see plaintext columns. A fitness refresh token is a standing key to someone's health data: the site's Withings guide quotes refresh tokens lasting a year, and Polar's documentation says in one section that access tokens will not expire unless revoked. Sealing the token columns with a key held in a separate secret store means a leak of the database alone leaks ciphertext, and the key can be rotated without touching the data's owner."
      },
      {
        "q": "What does binding a sealed token to its database row protect against?",
        "a": "Moving ciphertext around. Without a binding, anyone who can write to the token table can copy their own valid ciphertext onto another user's row, or a migration bug can shift tokens between users, and decryption succeeds either way. The recipe feeds a context, such as user id, vendor and field name, to AES-GCM as additional authenticated data. The context is not stored with the ciphertext; it has to be supplied again to open it, so a token opened under a different user id or field fails authentication. Node's documentation says that if the cipher text has been tampered with, decipher.final() will throw."
      },
      {
        "q": "How do you rotate the key that encrypts stored OAuth tokens without downtime?",
        "a": "Make every sealed value carry the id of the key that sealed it, then rotate in three steps: add the new key alongside the old one and make it the active key, so new seals use it while old rows still open; rewrap each old row under the new key as it is read, or in a background sweep; and delete the old key only when no row still names it. The recipe's needsRewrap and rewrap do the middle step, rewrap never writes plaintext, and opening a row whose key was deleted throws an unknown-key error, which is the signal that the sweep was not finished."
      }
    ],
