    "slug": "health-connect-error-policy",
    "primaryQuery": "health connect exception retry or ask user error handling",
    "h1": "Recipe: A Health Connect Error Policy: Retry, Ask, Fix or Report",
    "metaTitle": "Recipe: Health Connect Error Handling Policy",
    "metaDescription": "Tested code that maps every HealthConnectException code and Jetpack exception to retry, ask the user, fix the request or report, with Google's sentence.",
    "updated": "2026-11-05",
    "answer": "A small, dependency-free policy that maps every Health Connect failure to one of four actions: retry later, ask the user, fix the request, or report. It covers all nine HealthConnectException error codes on the platform API and the exceptions Jetpack's HealthConnectClient documents, and stores beside each mapping the sentence in Google's reference that justifies it: ERROR_IO says the call might succeed if repeated, ERROR_RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED that the caller should try again later, ERROR_SECURITY that a permission is missing. Its tests fail if Google adds a code the policy does not cover, and a RemoteException from deleteRecords or updateRecords is treated as a bad id, as Google documents.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Which Health Connect error codes are safe to retry?",
        "a": "Four, by Google's own wording as the site read it on October 3, 2026: ERROR_IO, which the reference says might succeed if repeated; ERROR_RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED, where the caller should try this api call again later; ERROR_DATA_SYNC_IN_PROGRESS, where the caller may try again later; and ERROR_REMOTE, an IPC error the reference likens to RemoteException. The other five are not fixed by waiting: ERROR_SECURITY needs the user to grant a permission, ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT needs a different request, and ERROR_UNKNOWN, ERROR_INTERNAL and ERROR_UNSUPPORTED_OPERATION are reported. That split is this site's reading of Google's sentences, not a table Google publishes."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why should a RemoteException from Health Connect deleteRecords not be retried?",
        "a": "Because Google documents it as a likely symptom of your own input. HealthConnectClient's Throws table for deleteRecords says that deleting by invalid identifiers, such as a non-existing identifier or deleting the same record multiple times, will result in IPC failure, and for updateRecords and readRecord that update with invalid identifiers will result in IPC failure. So on those methods a RemoteException may be your bad id rather than a transport hiccup, and retrying sends the same bad id again. The recipe's policyFor takes the method name and returns fix-request for a RemoteException from those three, while other methods' RemoteExceptions stay retryable."
      },
      {
        "q": "How should an app respond to a Health Connect SecurityException at runtime?",
        "a": "Route the user to a permission request rather than retrying. Google's HealthConnectClient reference documents SecurityException for requests with unpermitted access across reads, writes, aggregates and changes, and the platform code ERROR_SECURITY means the caller does not have the correct permissions for the call. A permission the user revoked in settings does not come back on its own, so a retry loop only burns rate limit and battery. The recipe classifies both as ask-user, and its retry wrapper rethrows them on the first attempt with the policy attached, so the caller can show the permission screen."
      }
    ],
