    "slug": "ble-heart-rate-parser",
    "primaryQuery": "parse bluetooth heart rate measurement 0x2a37 javascript",
    "h1": "Recipe: Parse a BLE Heart Rate Measurement (0x2A37)",
    "metaTitle": "Recipe: Parse BLE Heart Rate Measurement 0x2A37",
    "metaDescription": "Tested JavaScript that decodes Bluetooth Heart Rate Measurement notifications: bpm, sensor contact, energy and RR intervals, per the SIG's structure.",
    "updated": "2026-11-05",
    "answer": "A dependency-free JavaScript parser for the Bluetooth Heart Rate Measurement characteristic (0x2A37) that turns a notification into heart rate, sensor contact, energy expended and RR intervals, and throws on a value the flags do not account for. Bit 0 of the Flags octet picks a uint8 or uint16 heart rate, bits 1 and 2 report contact, bit 3 announces a uint16 Energy Expended and bit 4 RR intervals in units of 1/1024 second, all little-endian. Its tests check those bit positions against the Bluetooth SIG structure the site generates and run the parser against the site's structure-driven decoder for every flag combination.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Why is the sensor contact field in a heart rate measurement three-valued?",
        "a": "Because two bits encode it and they mean different things. In the Bluetooth SIG's structure, bit 2 of the Flags octet says Sensor Contact Supported and bit 1 says Sensor Contact detected. When bit 2 is clear the device does not report contact at all, which is not the same as reporting no contact, so a parser that returns a boolean turns not supported into false and makes a chest strap that never reports contact look permanently detached. The recipe returns detected, not detected or not supported, and treats bit 1 as meaningless whenever bit 2 is clear."
      },
      {
        "q": "How do you convert BLE RR-interval values to milliseconds?",
        "a": "Multiply by 1000 and divide by 1024. The Bluetooth SIG's structure says each RR-Interval value is represented by a uint16 with 1/1024 second as the unit, so a raw 1024 is exactly one second and a raw 890 is about 869 milliseconds. A notification can carry several RR values, as many as fit after the fields before them, and a device that never sets bit 4 of the Flags octet sends none, in which case heart rate variability cannot be computed from that device at all. The recipe returns both the raw values and the milliseconds."
      },
      {
        "q": "What unit is the Energy Expended field of a heart rate measurement in?",
        "a": "The Bluetooth SIG's structure gives it the unit org.bluetooth.unit.energy.joule, and the recipe reports the raw uint16 with that unit. The Web Bluetooth specification's own heart-rate example, which the site's heart-rate lab also quotes, comments its value of 750 as 750kJ. The two sources disagree on the scale, so the recipe does not convert: it returns the number and the SIG's unit, and you decide from your device's documentation. In practice most apps take energy from the platform's calorie fields rather than from this characteristic."
      }
    ],
