    "slug": "withings-notification-router",
    "primaryQuery": "withings notification appli callback handler code",
    "h1": "Recipe: Route Withings Notifications to the Right Data API Call",
    "metaTitle": "Recipe: Withings Notification Router, Tested",
    "metaDescription": "Tested Node code that checks a Withings notification's callback secret, parses its form body and names the Data API call each appli category needs.",
    "updated": "2026-11-05",
    "answer": "A dependency-free Node router for Withings notifications. It checks the secret query parameter Withings suggests you append to your callback URL, in constant time, since Withings sends no signature; parses the form-encoded body for userid, appli and the startdate and enddate range; and names the Data API call each category needs: Measure - Getmeas for weight, temperature and blood-pressure categories, Measure v2 activity calls for 16, Sleep v2 for 44, Heart v2 - List for 54, and nothing for 55. An unknown category is acknowledged and surfaced rather than dropped, and duplicate notifications collapse into one job key. Its table is tested against the site's webhook verifier.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "How do you authenticate a Withings notification when it carries no signature?",
        "a": "With a secret you put in the callback URL yourself. Withings' notification guide says to append a query parameter only you and Withings know when you set the callback URL, because Withings POSTs to that exact URL, and to reject any value that does not match; it suggests combining that with its published IP allowlist. So the check is a constant-time comparison of the token parameter against your configured secret before anything else is parsed. The recipe returns 403 for a missing or wrong secret, and refuses to match an empty configured secret, which would otherwise accept every request without a token."
      },
      {
        "q": "What should a Withings notification handler do when it sees an unfamiliar appli value?",
        "a": "Acknowledge it and log it, never drop it silently. Withings retries a failed notification in cycles over about five hours and cancels the subscription after 20 days of unresolved failures, so returning an error for a category you do not yet handle risks the whole subscription; and a notification you discard is not redelivered. The recipe returns a 200 with call set to null and a reason pointing at Withings' Payload and Categories page, and builds no job for it, so the unknown value shows up in your logs as something to route rather than disappearing."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why queue the Withings fetch instead of calling the Data API inside the notification handler?",
        "a": "Because the notification is the one thing you cannot get back. Withings says missed notifications are not redelivered and that you should backfill via the Data API using lastupdate, and its guide asks endpoints to return a 2xx within a few seconds. Fetching inline ties the acknowledgment to a slow upstream call, so a Data API hiccup becomes a failed delivery and a retry storm. Returning the call and the date range as a job, keyed by user, category and range so duplicates collapse, lets the handler answer immediately and lets the fetch be retried, replayed or rate-limited on its own."
      }
    ],
